<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Borba (newspaper)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Borba ('Struggle') was a Yugoslav communist newspaper founded in 1922 as an organ of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. During the uprising of 1941 the Partisans printed it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzice">Užice</a>, in the short-lived liberated territory known as the Republic of Užice. After the war it became one of the main official dailies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/borba-newspaper.jpg" alt="Borba (newspaper)" width="320" height="503" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Borba editorial board · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borba_br_1_19021922_strana1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first issue of Borba, a word meaning fight or combat, appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> on 19 February 1922, edited by Đuro Cvijić and Kamil Horvatin. The communist party had been banned since December 1920 but went on working in secret in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, and the paper spread information among its members, activists and sympathisers. Vladimir Ćopić, its editor from 1924, was soon arrested for his articles against the government. On 13 January 1929, a week after King Alexander proclaimed the 6 January Dictatorship, Borba was banned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the paper was published in the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzice">Užice</a>, and after the liberation it moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. It was the official gazette of the League of Communists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> until 1954 and then of the Socialist Alliance of Working People of Yugoslavia. From 1948 to 1987 it also appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>, and for a long time it alternated pages in Serbian Cyrillic and Gaj's Latin alphabet in the same edition.</p><p>Stanislav Staša Marinković, editor-in-chief from July 1986, made it one of the most liberal dailies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, and under Manojlo Vukotić its journalists opposed the war policies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic">Slobodan Milošević</a> in 1992 and 1993. In 1994 the government took the paper over overnight; 120 employees left, and under the new editor Dragutin Brčin it supported Milošević and lost almost its entire readership.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Former Borba journalists founded Naša borba soon after the takeover, and most of them established Danas in 1997. Vukotić started Blic in 1996, and Slavko Ćuruvija, his successor as editor, started Dnevni telegraf, which appeared until Ćuruvija's assassination in March 1999. Borba itself was bought in 2002 by the businessman Stanko Subotić and printed a few hundred copies a day. A redesigned paper appeared on 15 January 2009 but sold fewer than 3,000 copies a day and closed in October 2009. A monthly magazine under the name began in 2020, which former Borba journalists criticised.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Borba (newspaper)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Local people's liberation committees took over administration, schools and supplies, while the town's arms factory produced rifles and ammunition in an underground vault, and the Communist newspaper <b>Borba</b> was printed there.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-uzice">Republic of Užice</a></li></ul></section>
